1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,700
More than a hundred miles of highway across the sea.

2
00:00:04,701 --> 00:00:10,600
A freeway of spectacular bridges, including one of the longest in the world.

3
00:00:10,601 --> 00:00:14,500
An engineering feat once thought impossible.

4
00:00:14,501 --> 00:00:20,500
A road like no other, through the paradise of the Florida Keys.

5
00:00:20,501 --> 00:00:25,500
Now, the Overseas Highway on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:25,501 --> 00:00:39,501
At the tip of Florida, the southernmost part of the continental United States, lies the overseas highway.

7
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:51,200
It is 119 miles long and begins just south of Miami off the coastal edge of the peninsula.

8
00:00:51,201 --> 00:00:58,680
It then winds its way in a southwesterly direction, over the Florida Keys, and ends in the city

9
00:00:58,681 --> 00:01:00,520
of Key West.

10
00:01:00,521 --> 00:01:07,780
Anyone who's ever been on the overseas highway from Miami to Key West will tell you it's

11
00:01:07,781 --> 00:01:13,980
one of the most amazing drives on Earth, where you're really on a spider's web of trail

12
00:01:13,981 --> 00:01:19,160
across the ocean.

13
00:01:19,161 --> 00:01:24,300
What sets this highway apart from other roadways is that so much of it is over water, roughly

14
00:01:24,301 --> 00:01:25,300
20%.

15
00:01:25,301 --> 00:01:32,360
It features an astonishing 51 bridges.

16
00:01:32,361 --> 00:01:39,000
Running parallel to several of these bridges are remnants of a century-old railway.

17
00:01:39,001 --> 00:01:43,820
Today's modern overseas highway owes its legacy to it.

18
00:01:43,821 --> 00:01:50,500
The old bridges are now used as fishing piers and bike and footpaths.

19
00:01:50,501 --> 00:01:55,700
Next to one of these rail bridges stands one of the newest structures on the overseas highway,

20
00:01:55,701 --> 00:01:57,700
Seven Mile Bridge.

21
00:01:57,701 --> 00:02:04,940
The bridge is more than 35,000 feet long and spans 6.7 miles of water.

22
00:02:04,941 --> 00:02:11,040
It is one of the longest of its kind in the world and among the most spectacular.

23
00:02:11,041 --> 00:02:15,880
Its tallest point is 65 feet high, allowing sailboats to pass under.

24
00:02:15,881 --> 00:02:20,980
It gives me goosebumps every time I drive over it.

25
00:02:20,981 --> 00:02:25,280
It's quite a structure.

26
00:02:25,281 --> 00:02:32,600
The new bridge was constructed in 1982 by Meisner Marine Corporation and designed by

27
00:02:32,601 --> 00:02:37,980
Fig Engineering Group as a precast concrete segmental bridge.

28
00:02:37,981 --> 00:02:46,900
The superstructure of the bridge, or the top portion of the bridge, is composed of precast segments that are in this shape, like a box with a voided section.

29
00:02:49,540 --> 00:02:52,760
A bridge span is the distance between two piers.

30
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:57,040
On 7 Mile, there are 223 spans.

31
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:02,520
Each is 135 feet long and composed of 768-ton segments.

32
00:03:02,521 --> 00:03:13,020
the segments are match cast which means in the casting yard you set your forms around the

33
00:03:13,021 --> 00:03:19,140
previously cast segment and you cast the new segment right up against it so that you have

34
00:03:19,141 --> 00:03:28,680
a perfect match so you want to cast them together more than 1500 segments were cast in the yard 450

35
00:03:28,681 --> 00:03:35,900
miles away in Tampa Florida from there they were barged directly to the bridge

36
00:03:35,901 --> 00:03:43,160
site on a man-made platform near the bridges the segments were assembled

37
00:03:43,161 --> 00:03:49,340
bridge workers threaded the segments with thousands of feet of steel cable the

38
00:03:49,341 --> 00:03:55,000
cable was post tensioned that is stressed and locked in stress so that the entire

39
00:03:55,001 --> 00:04:00,440
bridge span is in compression. This bridge technology in an earlier form was used in

40
00:04:00,441 --> 00:04:05,680
Europe following World War II where a quick cost-effective bridge replacement

41
00:04:05,681 --> 00:04:11,240
scheme was needed to replace the bridges that were destroyed after the war.

42
00:04:11,241 --> 00:04:16,680
A barge carried a span underneath the bridge. It was lifted by a special

43
00:04:16,681 --> 00:04:23,120
overhead gantry and placed on the bridge's concrete foundation piers. And you

44
00:04:23,121 --> 00:04:26,820
You could build, with this overhead gantry system and the pre-casting technique that

45
00:04:26,821 --> 00:04:33,440
was used, three spans of completed bridge a week.

46
00:04:33,441 --> 00:04:39,480
Workers completed Seven Mile Bridge six months ahead of schedule in the summer of 1982.

47
00:04:39,481 --> 00:04:42,800
It took two and a half years.

48
00:04:42,801 --> 00:04:49,560
This bridge was a great solution for the Seven Mile location because of its speed of construction

49
00:04:49,561 --> 00:04:50,560
and cost savings.

50
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:56,880
The modern overseas highway may have been completed in 1982,

51
00:04:57,460 --> 00:05:01,280
but the Florida Keys it crosses over are millions of years old.

52
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:07,780
The Keys are actually hundreds of islands built up on the skeleton of an ancient coral reef

53
00:05:07,781 --> 00:05:10,400
and surrounded by mostly shallow water.

54
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:19,860
Up until the early 20th century, life in this region was rough and travel options were limited.

55
00:05:19,861 --> 00:05:24,040
Well, it was totally by sea or by foot.

56
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:27,120
You either walked in or you got in a boat, you rowed it,

57
00:05:27,180 --> 00:05:30,340
or if you had the correct wind in a sailboat, you'd sail.

58
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:36,480
Except for a few farmers and those who made their living off the sea,

59
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:38,820
not many people called this place home.

60
00:05:39,900 --> 00:05:41,900
There was nothing in the Keys at this time,

61
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:44,100
not in the middle Keys or the upper Keys.

62
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:46,800
These were bits of reef.

63
00:05:46,801 --> 00:05:50,760
There were a few settlers, hardy settlers.

64
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:55,340
There was a lot of smuggling of one sort and another.

65
00:05:55,700 --> 00:05:58,720
It was a wild and woolly place in those days,

66
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:02,640
and very, very few people living, maybe a couple hundred people up and down the Keys.

67
00:06:04,920 --> 00:06:07,360
The exception was Key West.

68
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:10,720
Boasting 20,000 residents in 1900,

69
00:06:11,300 --> 00:06:14,100
it was bigger than Miami or any other city in Florida.

70
00:06:14,101 --> 00:06:21,360
deep-sea fishing, sponge harvesting, and cigar manufacturing made it a thriving commercial

71
00:06:21,361 --> 00:06:22,061
center.

72
00:06:22,062 --> 00:06:28,220
It was also a popular coal refueling stop for steamships coming from South America

73
00:06:28,221 --> 00:06:30,050
and the Caribbean.

74
00:06:30,051 --> 00:06:36,860
But life on the Keys was about to change irrevocably, thanks to the

75
00:06:36,861 --> 00:06:40,220
vision of an oil baron named Henry Morrison Flagler.

76
00:06:42,220 --> 00:06:46,840
I think a lot of people know who John D. Rockefeller, for instance, is, but I don't

77
00:06:46,841 --> 00:06:51,530
think they know that Henry Flagler was once the partner of John D.

78
00:06:51,531 --> 00:06:52,880
Rockefeller and the

79
00:06:52,881 --> 00:06:56,060
brains, according to Rockefeller, behind Standard Oil.

80
00:06:58,180 --> 00:07:04,320
In 1870, the value of Flagler's shares in Standard Oil was more than $125 million.

81
00:07:06,860 --> 00:07:10,160
But his life took a dramatic turn when he fell for Florida.

82
00:07:11,260 --> 00:07:16,420
Flagler was a lot like any other Midwesterner who comes to Florida and looks around and sees the palms swaying

83
00:07:16,421 --> 00:07:18,780
and feels that warm breeze off the ocean and says,

84
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:21,920
a man would have to be a fool to want to live anywhere else in the world.

85
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:26,020
He got seduced away from oil, actually got tired of it.

86
00:07:26,021 --> 00:07:30,240
He came down here with a second wife on a honeymoon, fell in love,

87
00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:34,740
and began building hotels and then railroads to service these hotels.

88
00:07:34,741 --> 00:07:43,140
They were luxury hotels with state-of-the-art conveniences like bathrooms and running water in all rooms.

89
00:07:44,180 --> 00:07:46,920
He was really into hotels. That was what he was after.

90
00:07:47,440 --> 00:07:53,020
But to support hotels, you've got to have a transportation system where people can get to your hotels.

91
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:57,360
And he proceeded to buy up every railroad on the east coast of Florida.

92
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:02,960
Flagler created the Florida East Coast Railway Company.

93
00:08:02,961 --> 00:08:07,840
and New York's elite were soon traveling by rail to vacation in Florida.

94
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:18,280
His next venture would be inspired by news in 1901 of a Panama Canal project.

95
00:08:21,240 --> 00:08:24,760
Flagler reasoned that Key West would be the closest U.S. port to the canal.

96
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:30,180
All he had to do was find the best way to get cargo from Key West to the mainland.

97
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:35,980
He decided to build an overseas railroad.

98
00:08:37,040 --> 00:08:40,740
At the time, some of his contemporaries called it Flagler's Folly.

99
00:08:41,300 --> 00:08:45,000
He was told that he could never build a railroad over open ocean.

100
00:08:45,940 --> 00:08:47,340
But Flagler would try.

101
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:55,160
If successful, coastal Florida would be linked to Key West for the first time in history.

102
00:08:56,740 --> 00:09:02,080
Seven Mile Bridge contains more than 1,150 miles of steel cable.

103
00:09:02,081 --> 00:09:06,500
Nearly enough to span the distance between Key West, Florida and Dover, Delaware.

104
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:11,040
We now return to the Overseas Highway on Modern Marvels.

105
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:18,140
No other roadway in the United States is quite like the Overseas Highway.

106
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:23,960
Linking so many islands and traveling such distances directly above the sea.

107
00:09:23,961 --> 00:09:34,500
And yet the blueprint for this remarkable modern highway was conceived in the minds of young engineers born in the mid-19th century.

108
00:09:37,700 --> 00:09:42,960
It was oil baron Henry Flagler's Florida East Coast Railway, built in the early 1900s,

109
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:49,780
that not only provided much of the route of today's overseas highway, but also its foundation.

110
00:09:49,781 --> 00:09:56,180
most of the highway follows the same path that was virtually hacked out of the subtropical

111
00:09:56,181 --> 00:10:05,780
wilderness for the railroad it's 153 miles from miami to key west much of it over open water and

112
00:10:05,781 --> 00:10:12,340
what isn't over open water was essentially swamp and railroad engineers debated whether such a

113
00:10:12,341 --> 00:10:19,720
thing could be done flagler thought it best to go directly from miami to the very tip of the

114
00:10:19,721 --> 00:10:25,360
Everglades and then jump 30 miles south straight across open water to the Middle Keys.

115
00:10:27,220 --> 00:10:34,000
They sent out a survey party and they tracked across the Everglades and immediately were lost,

116
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:38,400
drank all their water, ate their food, were wandering about to die until they were discovered

117
00:10:38,401 --> 00:10:41,747
by Indians who were at a hunting party and led to safety.

118
00:10:41,748 --> 00:10:43,920
And William Crone, who was the man in

119
00:10:43,921 --> 00:10:48,900
charge of this foray came back to Flagler and said forget the Everglades.

120
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:55,580
Flagler's engineers settled on a route that would go from Miami to the town of Homestead.

121
00:10:57,320 --> 00:11:01,820
The railroad would then jump across to Key Largo and head southwest from there.

122
00:11:04,220 --> 00:11:08,480
The plan was to use landfill to cross the shallow inlets between keys

123
00:11:08,481 --> 00:11:11,400
and build bridges to span waters that were too deep.

124
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:21,999
Flagler wanted to use as much landfill as possible, but critics believed that blocking the channel between the Gulf and the Atlantic Ocean would have negative

125
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:23,380
environmental consequences.

126
00:11:26,020 --> 00:11:31,104
The U.S. government told him that he had to leave six miles open.

127
00:11:31,105 --> 00:11:37,440
He wound up leaving 17 open. So there's 17 miles of bridges and 20 miles of fill.

128
00:11:37,441 --> 00:11:41,440
So even in those days there were environmental concerns.

129
00:11:41,441 --> 00:11:44,440
And then the practical concerns came.

130
00:11:44,441 --> 00:11:50,440
How do you get a workforce down at the tip end of a country when there are no people?

131
00:11:50,441 --> 00:11:57,440
And how do you get supplies when you're hundreds and thousands of miles from sources of materiel?

132
00:11:59,440 --> 00:12:02,440
Flagler brought in all equipment.

133
00:12:02,441 --> 00:12:06,440
Construction materials, fuel, food, and labor.

134
00:12:06,441 --> 00:12:08,080
by steamship and rail.

135
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:13,560
His vision was strong, and his pockets were deep.

136
00:12:14,740 --> 00:12:20,460
At one time, Flagler had under contract every available steamship on the east coast of the United States.

137
00:12:22,900 --> 00:12:24,920
Even barrels of water were imported.

138
00:12:26,020 --> 00:12:29,100
Other than rainfall, there are no sources of fresh water in the Keys.

139
00:12:33,040 --> 00:12:35,080
Construction began in 1905.

140
00:12:35,081 --> 00:12:42,980
Parties of men worked on different parts of the route, earning what was considered a good wage, a dollar and 25 cents a day.

141
00:12:43,940 --> 00:12:45,680
But conditions were extremely harsh.

142
00:12:47,580 --> 00:12:53,300
Ten, twelve hour days, they worked hard for that dollar and 25 cents that they put in every day.

143
00:12:54,480 --> 00:12:57,480
A lot of men came from cities of the northeast.

144
00:12:57,481 --> 00:13:03,180
homeless people on the edge willing to go long distances and work on unfamiliar and difficult

145
00:13:03,181 --> 00:13:05,078
terrain for workman's wages.

146
00:13:05,079 --> 00:13:09,620
They would get down here and find out how difficult the work really

147
00:13:09,621 --> 00:13:10,321
was.

148
00:13:10,322 --> 00:13:16,680
The heat, the humidity, the isolation, the mosquitoes, the alligators, the hurricanes,

149
00:13:16,900 --> 00:13:20,960
and they would want to turn right around and go back up north.

150
00:13:20,961 --> 00:13:27,260
Three hurricanes hit the line while it was being built.

151
00:13:28,160 --> 00:13:32,620
The worst was in 1906, when 125 men were killed.

152
00:13:34,820 --> 00:13:38,360
But not even hurricanes could derail Flackler's efforts to finish the railroad.

153
00:13:40,120 --> 00:13:44,600
Mile by mile, workers built embankments for raised railroad beds

154
00:13:44,601 --> 00:13:49,320
and erected dozens of small bridges to span short gaps between keys.

155
00:13:50,960 --> 00:13:56,820
Yet the toughest work lay ahead, building bridges that would cross vast channels of open water.

156
00:13:58,680 --> 00:14:04,060
Flagler had become enamored of a new building material called reinforced concrete,

157
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:11,360
and he'd found the man in all the world most expert in the use of this material, Joseph Meredith.

158
00:14:12,360 --> 00:14:19,560
It was a lot like asking Werner von Braun if he'd like to take part in a rocket building project at the time.

159
00:14:20,960 --> 00:14:25,840
Meredith immediately accepted the invitation and began engineering the longer bridges.

160
00:14:26,880 --> 00:14:31,680
The first was Long Key Viaduct, which still stands next to the modern highway.

161
00:14:32,640 --> 00:14:39,260
It was designed to rest on 180 reinforced concrete arches, crossing more than two miles of open sea.

162
00:14:42,380 --> 00:14:46,660
In 1906, bridge workers started on the foundation piers.

163
00:14:47,640 --> 00:14:51,980
They drove wood pilings into the ocean floor using steam-powered pile drivers.

164
00:14:53,020 --> 00:14:59,600
Then they sank tall wooden forms around the pilings that would hold the water out and provide a dry construction area.

165
00:15:01,140 --> 00:15:02,740
They were called cofferdams.

166
00:15:05,020 --> 00:15:12,920
Next, they poured a special underwater concrete mix into the cofferdam to form a watertight seal two to five feet thick around the pilings.

167
00:15:12,921 --> 00:15:19,720
workers then used a pump to suck water out of the cofferdams and left the concrete seal to harden

168
00:15:19,721 --> 00:15:27,420
later they could climb inside the dry work area to affix a second more refined wooden form

169
00:15:27,421 --> 00:15:30,500
with a lattice-like network of reinforced steel

170
00:15:30,501 --> 00:15:36,980
this second form would be filled with more concrete to create a pier

171
00:15:36,981 --> 00:15:44,020
So you would now have a bunch of stubs, piers, out in the water.

172
00:15:44,880 --> 00:15:52,920
The walls, the frames for the arches, were then brought in and set on top of the cured concrete piers,

173
00:15:53,400 --> 00:15:55,540
bolted together with bolts and lag bolts.

174
00:15:57,640 --> 00:16:01,940
This wooden arch form created the arches of the bridge and the walls.

175
00:16:03,140 --> 00:16:05,080
Workers poured concrete inside.

176
00:16:06,020 --> 00:16:09,460
Once the concrete hardened, the wooden forms were removed.

177
00:16:10,400 --> 00:16:15,500
What was left were walls more than a foot thick on either side of the bridge

178
00:16:15,501 --> 00:16:18,580
and a camel-humped channel running between them.

179
00:16:19,980 --> 00:16:23,580
They filled the inside of those arches with gravel and rock

180
00:16:23,581 --> 00:16:27,020
so that the railroad did not set on concrete.

181
00:16:27,380 --> 00:16:28,700
There'd be too much vibration.

182
00:16:30,220 --> 00:16:32,460
Ties and track were then placed on top.

183
00:16:32,461 --> 00:16:41,200
If you're out there in a skiff at dawn or at evening, it looks like a giant Roman aqueduct marching across the ocean.

184
00:16:42,560 --> 00:16:45,560
Long Key Viaduct was completed in 1908.

185
00:16:46,760 --> 00:16:51,220
By then, enough track and bridges were finished so that passengers could board a train in Miami

186
00:16:51,221 --> 00:16:58,020
and travel 106 miles of the proposed 153-mile route to Key West.

187
00:16:58,021 --> 00:17:03,020
7 Mile Bridge was the next major undertaking.

188
00:17:03,021 --> 00:17:08,020
It was about 15 miles southwest of Long Key.

189
00:17:08,021 --> 00:17:13,020
Joseph Meredith incorporated two different styles into the design.

190
00:17:13,021 --> 00:17:19,020
The first would have tall concrete piers with a steel railroad truss.

191
00:17:19,021 --> 00:17:25,020
The second style would be the same as Long Key Viaduct with concrete arches.

192
00:17:25,021 --> 00:17:32,020
In 1909, workers used the same construction methods they used at Long Key.

193
00:17:32,021 --> 00:17:38,520
They drilled wood pilings into the ocean bottom and used cofferdams and reinforced concrete

194
00:17:38,521 --> 00:17:42,520
to create the bridge's 546 foundation piers.

195
00:17:42,521 --> 00:17:52,520
They jumped off land at the south end at Knights Key and went out to a little over a mile to

196
00:17:52,521 --> 00:17:55,020
fly speck of land called Pigeon Key.

197
00:17:57,920 --> 00:18:00,920
Seven Mile Bridge went directly over Pigeon Key.

198
00:18:02,860 --> 00:18:06,580
Its great piers rested on the island before continuing on over water.

199
00:18:08,420 --> 00:18:11,160
The island was also used as a labor base camp.

200
00:18:12,120 --> 00:18:15,180
Today, it's a museum and marine science camp for students.

201
00:18:17,200 --> 00:18:21,640
All this lovely vegetation that you see on the island today simply didn't exist.

202
00:18:21,641 --> 00:18:24,140
It was a barren coral rock.

203
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:30,260
From Pigeon Key, the bridge went southward over five miles of open water.

204
00:18:31,460 --> 00:18:39,900
It was the most massive, single railroad bridge undertaking in the United States up to that time,

205
00:18:39,980 --> 00:18:41,360
and certainly one of the most daunting.

206
00:18:42,560 --> 00:18:44,720
Seven Mile took three years to finish.

207
00:18:46,780 --> 00:18:49,880
The last challenge was the Bahia Honda Bridge.

208
00:18:51,640 --> 00:18:54,700
It was less than 10 miles southwest of Seven Mile Bridge,

209
00:18:54,900 --> 00:18:57,460
and the distance it would span was little more than a mile.

210
00:18:58,380 --> 00:19:02,500
But the waters below were 30 to 35 feet deep,

211
00:19:03,080 --> 00:19:06,780
at least 10 feet deeper than Long Key or Seven Mile Bridge,

212
00:19:07,300 --> 00:19:09,460
and the deepest anywhere along the route.

213
00:19:10,540 --> 00:19:12,480
That bridge had to be the highest.

214
00:19:12,680 --> 00:19:16,160
If the storm is strong enough in a 35-foot channel,

215
00:19:16,300 --> 00:19:19,440
you could have a 35-foot tidal wave being swept up.

216
00:19:21,640 --> 00:19:27,220
Workers were challenged to drive pilings deep enough into the coral rock to create a stable foundation.

217
00:19:28,420 --> 00:19:32,280
They then built a steel railroad trestle on top of the concrete piers.

218
00:19:33,360 --> 00:19:35,700
The bridge was completed in 1912.

219
00:19:36,480 --> 00:19:44,880
What I marvel at is what the early builders of the bridge, the older bridges, did considering the hardships that they had to suffer in building that.

220
00:19:44,960 --> 00:19:48,420
They didn't have the computational power that we have now.

221
00:19:48,421 --> 00:19:57,000
They didn't have the understanding of materials, and they didn't have the capabilities with materials, modern materials, that we can use in construction.

222
00:19:59,580 --> 00:20:08,200
Henry Morrison Flagler's Overseas Railroad was finally finished on January 21, 1912, after seven years of construction.

223
00:20:10,320 --> 00:20:12,920
Key West was no longer a distant outpost.

224
00:20:13,880 --> 00:20:15,960
It was an amazing feat.

225
00:20:16,140 --> 00:20:20,280
Imagine the largest city in Florida, and you couldn't get there except by boat.

226
00:20:20,460 --> 00:20:22,780
Now finally reachable by rail.

227
00:20:25,440 --> 00:20:30,220
It's estimated that the total cost of the railroad was somewhere between $30 and $50 million.

228
00:20:31,520 --> 00:20:34,000
Flagler used his own money to finance it.

229
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:40,580
Thousands of men worked on the railroad, and more than 100 died.

230
00:20:42,920 --> 00:20:49,920
Flagler himself died in 1913, a year after the Overseas Railroad was built.

231
00:20:49,921 --> 00:20:59,920
What he did was stitch the last little piece of the American continent to the main and

232
00:20:59,921 --> 00:21:02,920
truly close the American frontier.

233
00:21:02,921 --> 00:21:09,920
The Overseas Railroad would enjoy extensive use until it stood in the path of one of the

234
00:21:09,921 --> 00:21:11,920
storms in American history.

235
00:21:11,921 --> 00:21:18,620
Old Seven Mile Bridge was the site of huge action sequences in the James Bond film License

236
00:21:18,621 --> 00:21:21,620
to Kill and in True Lies with Arnold Schwarzenegger.

237
00:21:21,621 --> 00:21:30,620
Journalists in the 1910s called the Overseas Railroad the eighth wonder of the world.

238
00:21:30,621 --> 00:21:35,620
Its bridges were hailed as engineering marvels.

239
00:21:35,621 --> 00:21:42,420
Residents who once could only travel between islands by boat now found an easy way to reach

240
00:21:42,421 --> 00:21:45,740
both ends of the Keys.

241
00:21:45,741 --> 00:21:49,980
People in Miami looked south and there was another large city not too far away with good

242
00:21:49,981 --> 00:21:52,620
fishing and they wanted to go there.

243
00:21:52,621 --> 00:21:57,060
The Florida East Coast Railway would make little money from passenger traffic.

244
00:21:57,061 --> 00:22:01,660
The real money was in freight.

245
00:22:01,661 --> 00:22:08,740
But expectations of a flood of imported goods from the Panama Canal never materialized.

246
00:22:08,741 --> 00:22:16,000
Steamship technology had changed, eliminating the need for coal refueling stops at Key West.

247
00:22:16,001 --> 00:22:21,340
Steamships had been refitted with boilers that used a different type of fuel, and that

248
00:22:21,341 --> 00:22:22,340
was oil.

249
00:22:22,341 --> 00:22:28,520
So a steamship could take on enough oil as fuel passing through the Panama Canal and

250
00:22:28,521 --> 00:22:34,220
and then sailed merrily pretty much anywhere it wanted without the need for an intermediary stop in Key West.

251
00:22:35,500 --> 00:22:39,160
As a result, the overseas railroad never made much money,

252
00:22:40,240 --> 00:22:42,280
but residents found the train invaluable.

253
00:22:44,820 --> 00:22:53,240
It provided a transportation artery to bring artists, to bring visitors, to bring freight, and so forth.

254
00:22:53,241 --> 00:22:56,440
But it did seem to actually change the culture.

255
00:22:56,441 --> 00:23:00,780
I guess it's like opening the door to something new.

256
00:23:03,500 --> 00:23:06,840
With the railroad came a growing network of roads linking it.

257
00:23:08,380 --> 00:23:10,680
Farmers needed to get their produce to the stations,

258
00:23:11,360 --> 00:23:14,400
and innkeepers needed to get tourists to their resorts.

259
00:23:16,120 --> 00:23:20,960
But the biggest spur to road development was the Florida land boom of the 1920s.

260
00:23:22,560 --> 00:23:26,200
Residents on the Keys were more than willing to join in the real estate frenzy.

261
00:23:26,440 --> 00:23:36,880
The temptation to sell a lot of the swamp land that we had here on Key Largo and the upper Keys and throughout the Keys was too big a temptation.

262
00:23:38,820 --> 00:23:45,740
To encourage development, Monroe County, the county representing the Keys, issued bonds for further road expansion.

263
00:23:47,320 --> 00:23:54,940
They started out with probably a gravel or crushed shell base, and they braided it and drained it.

264
00:23:54,941 --> 00:23:57,880
And in 1920s, that was a wonderful road.

265
00:23:58,420 --> 00:24:00,940
It didn't get muddy in the rain, and you could drive on it.

266
00:24:03,500 --> 00:24:09,160
By 1928, motorists could travel on a small network of roads and wooden bridges

267
00:24:09,161 --> 00:24:12,900
running parallel to the railroad and linking parts of the Keys.

268
00:24:13,840 --> 00:24:17,740
But there was still no way to drive directly from the mainland to Key West.

269
00:24:19,640 --> 00:24:22,160
Drivers had to take a 40-mile ferry trip.

270
00:24:22,161 --> 00:24:28,760
Each ferryboat could take 20 cars, and if there were trucks aboard, it could even take

271
00:24:28,761 --> 00:24:30,760
less total vehicles.

272
00:24:30,761 --> 00:24:36,840
The $3.50 fare was considered expensive for the four-hour trip.

273
00:24:36,841 --> 00:24:42,960
Even worse, the ferries often ran aground.

274
00:24:42,961 --> 00:24:46,860
Monroe County launched a study to determine what it would cost to get rid of the ferry

275
00:24:46,861 --> 00:24:50,060
system and build a complete overseas highway.

276
00:24:50,061 --> 00:24:59,520
But in 1929, within a year of the study, the stock market crashed and Monroe County was

277
00:24:59,521 --> 00:25:00,520
broke.

278
00:25:00,521 --> 00:25:04,820
The county turned to the U.S. Congress for help with its highway project.

279
00:25:04,821 --> 00:25:09,620
But they weren't the only ones looking for assistance.

280
00:25:09,621 --> 00:25:17,200
In 1932, 20,000 World War I veterans and their families marched on Washington to demand early

281
00:25:17,201 --> 00:25:22,260
payment of their war pensions. They were called the Bonus Army.

282
00:25:26,360 --> 00:25:34,400
The vets were due to receive $1.25 for every day served overseas and $1 for every day served at

283
00:25:34,401 --> 00:25:36,598
home. But not until 1945.

284
00:25:36,599 --> 00:25:41,960
The Depression was on and they couldn't wait for their money.

285
00:25:41,961 --> 00:25:49,320
And it was a peaceful petitioning until the government finally intervened and they had tanks and tear gas.

286
00:25:49,680 --> 00:25:53,400
And it was an ugly scene both politically as well as socially.

287
00:25:53,740 --> 00:25:55,480
And they were successful at moving them out.

288
00:25:56,020 --> 00:25:57,320
They did not solve the problem.

289
00:25:58,500 --> 00:25:59,581
When Franklin D.

290
00:25:59,582 --> 00:26:05,800
Roosevelt took office in 1933, he found a way to help both Monroe County and the Bonus Army.

291
00:26:05,801 --> 00:26:12,800
He offered the vets the chance to work in a New Deal public works program.

292
00:26:12,801 --> 00:26:19,800
They would earn a dollar a day at various locations throughout the United States, including the Florida Keys.

293
00:26:19,801 --> 00:26:27,800
By October 1934, more than 700 vets were living in three work camps in the Keys,

294
00:26:27,801 --> 00:26:31,800
building Monroe County's overseas highway project.

295
00:26:31,801 --> 00:26:32,501
project.

296
00:26:32,502 --> 00:26:38,800
Their face would be determined by a natural catastrophe of biblical proportions.

297
00:26:41,800 --> 00:26:47,800
In 1935, just before Labor Day weekend, one of the most powerful storms ever recorded

298
00:26:47,801 --> 00:26:52,800
in the United States was building force in the Atlantic Ocean.

299
00:26:52,801 --> 00:27:00,800
The betterment were working along very well and along in August they get word of a hurricane

300
00:27:00,801 --> 00:27:04,320
brewing out in the Bahamas, and it was a long ways off.

301
00:27:05,960 --> 00:27:09,860
It turned out, though, that in making a curious bend,

302
00:27:10,220 --> 00:27:14,400
it lingered over the warm waters of the Straits and picked up steam.

303
00:27:15,400 --> 00:27:21,480
By Sunday, September 2, 1935, the hurricane was heading straight for the Keys.

304
00:27:21,481 --> 00:27:32,340
you had a number of residents and highway workers housed in tents on land about seven to nine feet

305
00:27:32,341 --> 00:27:42,300
above sea level directly in the path of this huge storm coming in well when officials of the work

306
00:27:42,301 --> 00:27:51,000
camp realized how bad it was they sent out a plea for help to miami the federal government authorized

307
00:27:51,001 --> 00:27:55,680
rescue train but because of a series of bureaucratic delays and technical

308
00:27:55,681 --> 00:28:02,100
mishaps the train didn't reach the keys until evening the railroad was in now

309
00:28:02,101 --> 00:28:07,560
receivership it was bankrupt so it was not simple as it used to be when flagler

310
00:28:07,561 --> 00:28:13,740
had it you had to go through the legal manipulations it took some time to do

311
00:28:13,741 --> 00:28:15,880
this

312
00:28:15,881 --> 00:28:22,500
When the train finally arrived in the middle keys where the vets were, the hurricane was in full force.

313
00:28:23,680 --> 00:28:31,360
The train was hammered by hard driving rain, rising water, and cyclonic force winds of 200 miles an hour.

314
00:28:33,500 --> 00:28:39,040
It was a Category 5 hurricane, the strongest on record at the time to hit American shores.

315
00:28:40,980 --> 00:28:43,200
You would not have been able to walk around.

316
00:28:43,201 --> 00:28:48,300
You would have had to have crawled around on all fours to reduce the wind resistance.

317
00:28:48,620 --> 00:28:49,880
The sound is horrible.

318
00:28:51,440 --> 00:28:55,740
The rescue train was stalled halfway down the keys at the Isla Morata station

319
00:28:55,741 --> 00:28:57,500
after picking up some of the stranded.

320
00:29:00,900 --> 00:29:05,580
Then, just after 8 p.m., it was hit by a 20-foot wall of water,

321
00:29:06,120 --> 00:29:07,860
pushed ashore by the storm's fury.

322
00:29:07,861 --> 00:29:19,000
The train's 320,000-pound cab survived, but its passenger cars were swept off the tracks and crushed.

323
00:29:20,620 --> 00:29:26,140
Miraculously, there were survivors, but the overall loss of life that night was extensive.

324
00:29:30,820 --> 00:29:37,160
In an area of the Keys where no more than 1,000 people lived, it's estimated that more than half were killed.

325
00:29:37,860 --> 00:29:40,520
Many were the World War I vets.

326
00:29:42,220 --> 00:29:45,000
Record keeping wasn't then what it is today.

327
00:29:45,740 --> 00:29:52,840
Estimates vary wildly, but somewhere between 500 and 800 people were assumed to have been drowned

328
00:29:52,841 --> 00:29:55,960
as that storm swept through the Middle Keys that day.

329
00:29:56,980 --> 00:30:03,780
Corpses were found for days in trees, floating in water, buried inside cars, and under rubble.

330
00:30:03,781 --> 00:30:11,760
people were impaled by by limbs and decapitated by flying debris i mean it was just a real horror

331
00:30:11,761 --> 00:30:20,480
show damage was extensive 40 miles of railroad tracks swept away simply obliterated destroyed

332
00:30:20,481 --> 00:30:27,460
three to four miles of railroad embankments were destroyed and patches of road were wiped out

333
00:30:27,461 --> 00:30:32,580
surprisingly what did remain standing were henry flagler's railway bridges

334
00:30:32,581 --> 00:30:39,580
But the Florida East Coast Railway was in no position to rebuild its crippled line.

335
00:30:39,581 --> 00:30:48,580
The state government stepped in, and for $640,000, acquired the railroad and its right-of-way.

336
00:30:48,581 --> 00:30:54,580
The state of Florida now faced a decision.

337
00:30:54,581 --> 00:30:59,580
Resurrect the railroad, or find a way to build an overseas highway

338
00:30:59,581 --> 00:31:05,580
an overseas highway that would link the roads that survived north and south of the hurricane's path.

339
00:31:06,580 --> 00:31:10,580
While the longest span on the overseas highway is Seven Mile Bridge,

340
00:31:10,581 --> 00:31:14,580
the shortest is Harris Gap Bridge at 110 feet.

341
00:31:17,580 --> 00:31:22,580
Henry Flagler's railroad bridges survived the hurricane in 1935.

342
00:31:23,580 --> 00:31:26,580
But 40 miles of railroad track were gone,

343
00:31:26,581 --> 00:31:29,960
leaving Key West once again cut off from the mainland.

344
00:31:32,360 --> 00:31:36,020
Residents were divided over whether the state of Florida should rebuild the railroad

345
00:31:36,021 --> 00:31:38,800
or construct a new highway.

346
00:31:39,800 --> 00:31:43,680
If they were going to build the highway, they had perfectly good bridges right there.

347
00:31:43,681 --> 00:31:49,380
They didn't have to go out and sink new piles or build new footings or do anything.

348
00:31:49,500 --> 00:31:54,020
All they had to do was figure out how to use Mr. Flagler's existing bridges.

349
00:31:54,021 --> 00:32:01,020
The automobile won out, and legislators went with the most economical solution.

350
00:32:01,021 --> 00:32:07,020
They would build the overseas highway using the old train path and Henry Flagler's bridges.

351
00:32:07,021 --> 00:32:14,020
Work to transform the overseas railroad into a highway began in November 1936.

352
00:32:14,021 --> 00:32:21,020
It was concentrated on the Middle Keys, where the hurricane had done its worst damage.

353
00:32:21,021 --> 00:32:29,020
On land, it was simply a matter of removing existing railroad track, widening the rail beds,

354
00:32:29,021 --> 00:32:36,020
laying in a crushed rock gravel foundation, and pouring concrete and paving it.

355
00:32:36,021 --> 00:32:41,020
It wasn't much different than paving over a country dirt road.

356
00:32:41,021 --> 00:32:47,020
However, converting the many railroad bridges to accommodate the automobile traffic presented

357
00:32:47,021 --> 00:32:50,220
presented an entirely different, more complicated problem.

358
00:32:51,880 --> 00:32:58,000
The easiest thing to do was to widen the top of the bridge for two-way traffic.

359
00:32:58,080 --> 00:33:06,600
So they put cross beams, steel girders, across the track to make a 20-foot wide bridge.

360
00:33:07,440 --> 00:33:16,160
So between 1935 and 1938, they went around to each pier and patched it up, as the case may be,

361
00:33:16,161 --> 00:33:18,280
whatever concrete work needed to be done.

362
00:33:18,640 --> 00:33:20,620
They took away the old railroad equipment,

363
00:33:21,300 --> 00:33:26,640
and in its place, they laid down across the original steel beam

364
00:33:26,641 --> 00:33:29,540
so they could put down pre-stressed concrete decking.

365
00:33:31,120 --> 00:33:35,920
Each bridge was given two small 9-inch curbs and guardrails.

366
00:33:36,760 --> 00:33:39,860
They actually built in steel rails,

367
00:33:40,020 --> 00:33:43,040
and you can still see some as you drive by the old bridges,

368
00:33:43,560 --> 00:33:45,300
but they're pretty much rusted away now.

369
00:33:45,301 --> 00:33:51,180
But the rails on the bridges were made from actual railroad tracks taken from the train.

370
00:33:54,120 --> 00:34:00,960
Placing a concrete deck on top of the railroad tracks worked well for all of the overseas bridges with the exception of one.

371
00:34:02,240 --> 00:34:03,540
The Bahia Honda Bridge.

372
00:34:04,360 --> 00:34:10,780
Most of the arch-top bridges could simply have the top platform widened for a two-lane highway.

373
00:34:10,781 --> 00:34:23,340
But when they got to the Bahia Honda Bridge, which is a typical steel camelback, steel suspension type bridge, it was only wide enough for the train to go through.

374
00:34:25,380 --> 00:34:29,100
With this bridge design, there was no room for crossbeams inside.

375
00:34:29,101 --> 00:34:34,400
As a matter of fact, the conductors used to come by when they go through the Bahia Honda Bridge

376
00:34:34,401 --> 00:34:39,220
and close all the windows so someone would not be tempted to stick their hands out the window

377
00:34:39,221 --> 00:34:41,360
and possibly get a hand knocked off.

378
00:34:43,900 --> 00:34:48,520
The trestles could be dismantled, but road engineers thought of a cheaper solution.

379
00:34:50,620 --> 00:34:53,800
They would build a concrete deck on top of the trestles.

380
00:34:53,801 --> 00:35:05,700
workers threw steel I-beams across the top of the bridge, built a platform, and poured and paved a reinforced concrete road on the platform.

381
00:35:07,800 --> 00:35:13,160
On March 29, 1938, the overseas highway opened to traffic.

382
00:35:14,740 --> 00:35:21,220
During its first days of operation, several thousand cars traveled the distance between Miami and Key West.

383
00:35:23,800 --> 00:35:27,040
World War II brought even more changes to the Florida Highway.

384
00:35:28,280 --> 00:35:32,380
Key West was an important Navy base with 4,000 naval personnel.

385
00:35:33,440 --> 00:35:36,200
And better roads were needed to transport troops and equipment.

386
00:35:38,620 --> 00:35:43,120
The federal government upgraded the old roads and auto bridges from the 1920s.

387
00:35:44,060 --> 00:35:46,460
They also shortened the overseas highway route.

388
00:35:48,660 --> 00:35:51,660
The most dramatic shortcut was at card sound.

389
00:35:51,661 --> 00:35:57,900
Drivers were crossing over from the mainland to Key Largo using narrow roads and bridges

390
00:35:57,901 --> 00:36:02,700
built in 1928.

391
00:36:02,701 --> 00:36:07,520
The Army Corps of Engineers bypassed this old section by using the path Flagler's railroad

392
00:36:07,521 --> 00:36:13,680
used as it left mainland Florida.

393
00:36:13,681 --> 00:36:18,280
The section linked up with the highway system already established on the Keys and cut travel

394
00:36:18,281 --> 00:36:20,380
to Key West by 17 miles.

395
00:36:22,780 --> 00:36:29,180
In 1944, the government designated the improved overseas highway U.S. Route 1.

396
00:36:31,680 --> 00:36:34,960
Since federal money was used, the federal government, you know,

397
00:36:35,100 --> 00:36:37,740
named it as part of the federal highway system.

398
00:36:39,080 --> 00:36:45,180
Motorists could now drive 2,200 miles directly from Fort Kent, Maine to Key West.

399
00:36:45,181 --> 00:36:50,080
However, the Overseas Highway was far from finished.

400
00:36:50,081 --> 00:36:58,420
More work, more men, and more money would be called on to make the Overseas Highway truly

401
00:36:58,421 --> 00:36:59,420
spectacular.

402
00:36:59,421 --> 00:37:06,940
According to the Florida Department of Transportation, as many as 42,000 cars travel on the Overseas

403
00:37:06,941 --> 00:37:10,680
Highway each day.

404
00:37:10,681 --> 00:37:15,280
its development, the overseas highway stood as one of the most astonishing roadways in

405
00:37:15,281 --> 00:37:17,060
the United States.

406
00:37:17,061 --> 00:37:23,420
But by the 1970s, the highway was showing signs of wear.

407
00:37:23,421 --> 00:37:26,240
Modern day traffic was taking its toll.

408
00:37:26,241 --> 00:37:30,280
Vehicle travel was also much more prominent.

409
00:37:30,281 --> 00:37:33,520
The cars were changing, speeds were greater.

410
00:37:33,521 --> 00:37:39,340
Then we noticed in 1970 that parts of the Bahia Honda overhead portion began to crack

411
00:37:39,341 --> 00:37:41,980
in Cromwell and falling into the water below.

412
00:37:43,920 --> 00:37:46,520
The Florida Department of Transportation investigated.

413
00:37:47,620 --> 00:37:49,760
They found out that it was severely corroded

414
00:37:50,320 --> 00:37:52,960
and also your lane width per vehicle was somewhat limited.

415
00:37:52,961 --> 00:37:55,560
What you were having is a lot of vehicles going down in the keys

416
00:37:55,561 --> 00:37:57,920
were passing each other very close.

417
00:37:57,921 --> 00:38:01,520
Frequently, you had instances where mirrors would slap each other on vehicles.

418
00:38:03,000 --> 00:38:04,660
It was time for new bridges.

419
00:38:04,661 --> 00:38:13,660
From 1978 to 1982, the state embarked on a $175 million program to replace bridges in

420
00:38:13,661 --> 00:38:19,660
the Keys with wider, more modern structures.

421
00:38:19,661 --> 00:38:22,660
Bahia Honda was the first major bridge project.

422
00:38:22,661 --> 00:38:27,660
The Department of Transportation built the highway's only four-lane bridge.

423
00:38:27,661 --> 00:38:33,660
It's a concrete highway on concrete foundation piers.

424
00:38:33,661 --> 00:38:38,260
Seven Mile Bridge, Long Key and others followed.

425
00:38:38,261 --> 00:38:43,120
Long Key Bridge and two smaller bridges used the same segmented bridge technology as the

426
00:38:43,121 --> 00:38:45,660
modern Seven Mile Bridge.

427
00:38:45,661 --> 00:38:51,840
They used precast concrete segments and post-tension steel.

428
00:38:51,841 --> 00:38:57,560
Seven Mile in particular became a model for other bridge projects outside of Florida.

429
00:38:57,561 --> 00:38:59,560
Builders flocked to see it.

430
00:38:59,561 --> 00:39:04,980
Many engineers and bridge owners came from around the world to visit the bridge during

431
00:39:04,981 --> 00:39:05,980
construction.

432
00:39:05,981 --> 00:39:14,280
It's just miles and miles of bridge with beautiful scenery on both sides.

433
00:39:14,281 --> 00:39:17,020
You don't see much driving over it.

434
00:39:17,021 --> 00:39:23,440
Riding alongside it or under it, you get to see what it really is.

435
00:39:23,441 --> 00:39:29,440
Seven Mile Bridge, Mahia Honda, Long Key and 34 other bridges were built or upgraded along

436
00:39:29,441 --> 00:39:36,440
the overseas highway. By 1982, the entire bridge replacement project was complete.

437
00:39:38,840 --> 00:39:43,820
Today, to ensure that these structures remain strong and safe, each bridge is thoroughly

438
00:39:43,821 --> 00:39:46,343
inspected once every two years.

439
00:39:46,344 --> 00:39:50,820
However, for a bridge like Seven Mile that's exposed to

440
00:39:51,560 --> 00:39:57,920
open water, there's steadfast vigilance for corrosion.

441
00:39:57,921 --> 00:40:01,520
takes about a month to complete.

442
00:40:01,521 --> 00:40:05,020
The Seven Mile Bridge is in a very corrosive atmosphere.

443
00:40:05,021 --> 00:40:07,780
It's in a saltwater environment essentially.

444
00:40:07,781 --> 00:40:12,920
Chlorides in the salt waters are picked up and deposited on the bridge due to wind action.

445
00:40:12,921 --> 00:40:18,320
Rainfall causes the chloride to drip down into cracks and into crevices and eventually

446
00:40:18,321 --> 00:40:20,720
they can cause damage to the bridge structure.

447
00:40:20,721 --> 00:40:26,780
But how do you inspect a bridge that's miles away from shore and more than 60 feet above

448
00:40:26,781 --> 00:40:36,380
water you use a snooper we pull the snooper next to the guardrail on the on the segmental bridge

449
00:40:36,381 --> 00:40:41,840
